    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**, all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` on owner creation \u2014 the number of existing owners (before this create) that already share the new owner's `firstName` and `lastName`, compared case-insensitively.\n\nThe endpoint is served through OfficeFloor function injection, so I followed that pipeline pattern rather than a Spring controller:\n\n1. **New pipeline step** \u2014 `CountNamesakes.java` (in `.../rest/function/owner/`). It reads the built `@Val Owner`, iterates `ownerRepository.findAll()`, and counts existing owners whose `firstName` and `lastName` both match case-insensitively (`equalsIgnoreCase`, null-safe), then calls `owner.setNamesakeCount(count)`. It runs **before** `SaveOwner`, so `findAll()` sees only pre-existing owners \u2014 a unique name yields 0.\n\n2. **Wiring** \u2014 inserted `countNamesakes` into `owners.POST.yml` between `assignHousehold` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\n3. **Persistence** \u2014 added a `namesakeCount` (`Integer`, column `namesake_count`) field + getter/setter to the `Owner` entity, so `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns the stored value. Added the `namesake_count` column to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres).\n\n4. **API/DTO** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml`; the generated `OwnerDto` picks it up and MapStruct's `OwnerMapper` auto-maps it by name (both entity and DTO), so both the create response and the GET response carry it.\n\nThe provided `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` (two owners sharing `firstName`+`lastName`, asserting `$.namesakeCount == 1` on the second) passes, and no other tests regressed.",
